Welcome to the first notebook in this Scala 3 track. The goal of this session is small but solid. By the end of it you will know what Scala is, why it earns its place on the java virtual machine, how to install Scala 3 on your machine, and how to run your first lines of code in three different ways. Prior programming experience helps but it is not required. If you have written Python or Java, you will see familiar shapes here, just dressed in slightly different clothing.

Let us start with the simplest possible question. What is Scala. Scala is a statically typed, hybrid functional and object oriented language that runs on the java virtual machine. That single sentence is dense, so let us unpack it.

Statically typed means every value has a type that the compiler knows at compile time. If you try to add a string and an integer, the compiler stops you before the program ever runs. This is the opposite of Python, where the same mistake only blows up at runtime when the offending line actually executes.

Hybrid functional and object oriented means Scala is fluent in two paradigms. You can write classes and objects the way you would in Java. You can also write pure functions, pass them around as values, and chain transformations on collections the way you would in Haskell or in modern Python. Most real Scala code mixes the two on purpose. Classes model the nouns of your domain. Functions describe the verbs.

Runs on the java virtual machine means Scala compiles to the same bytecode Java compiles to. The same garbage collector, the same just in time compiler, the same threading model, and crucially the same ecosystem. Every Java library, every Kafka client, every Hadoop tool, every database driver on Maven Central is available to you the moment you start a Scala project. You inherit twenty five years of mature tooling for free.

Scala was created by Martin Odersky and his team at EPFL in 2004. The name is a contraction of the words scalable language. The idea was that the same syntax should let you write a five line script and a five million line system, with no rewrite required as your code grows. That ambition is what shaped the language.

Four traits define Scala in one breath. It is statically typed but inference heavy, which means the compiler catches type errors yet you rarely need to spell the types out yourself. Functions are values, which means you can pass them, return them, and store them in collections just like you would an integer. Immutable is the default. The keyword val, short for value, binds something that cannot be reassigned. The keyword var binds something that can. In idiomatic Scala you reach for val first and only use var when there is a clear reason. And finally it runs on the java virtual machine, so the entire Java ecosystem is part of your standard library.

Now, why Scala in 2026. There are three reasons people pick this language today. The first is Spark and big data. Apache Spark itself is written in Scala. The native Spark application programming interface, the internals of the optimiser, the tuning knobs in the configuration system, are all expressed in Scala first. Python bindings exist, but they are bindings. The native shape of the system is Scala shaped.

The second reason is type safe domain modeling. Algebraic data types, pattern matching, and a powerful type system let you encode invariants the compiler will enforce for you. You can make illegal states unrepresentable. The compiler refuses to compile programs where a payment can be both pending and refunded at the same time. This is hard to express in mainstream languages, and Scala makes it natural.

The third reason is the java virtual machine ecosystem with functional programming ergonomics. You get every Java library plus map, flatMap, and pattern matching as first class citizens. For this learning track, the Spark connection is the centre of gravity. From here on, where it is natural, every notebook will point out the Scala feature being used inside Spark's own codebase.

A short word on versions. This track uses Scala 3, the version line that started in 2021. Its internal codename is Dotty. Scala 3 is a deep redesign of the language. The target is the same java virtual machine. The flavour is the same. But the type system underneath got cleaned up, and the surface syntax got much friendlier.

Two highlights you will feel immediately. The first is significant indentation. Scala 3 lets you express code blocks with indentation, the way Python does, instead of curly braces. Braces still work. You can choose per file. The second highlight is the new keywords given and using, which replace Scala 2's implicit keyword. Implicit was Scala 2's most powerful and also most confusing feature. Scala 3 rebuilt it with clearer keywords and clearer rules. You will meet given and using properly in notebook eleven.

If you ever land on a Stack Overflow answer that uses implicit val foo, that is Scala 2 vintage. The shape of the idea is unchanged. Only the keyword differs.

Let us spend a minute on what happens when you run Scala code. When you write a Scala source file, the compiler is a program called scalac. The compiler turns your source code into java virtual machine bytecode. That bytecode is exactly the same instruction set that Java compiles to. The java virtual machine then loads the bytecode, executes it, garbage collects unused memory, and uses its just in time compiler to optimise the hot paths in your program as it runs.

Two consequences are worth absorbing now. The first is that startup is not instant. The java virtual machine itself takes a moment to boot. For tiny one off scripts this feels slow. For long running services it disappears into the noise. The second consequence is that you inherit the java virtual machine's strengths. A mature garbage collector. A just in time compiler that gets faster the longer your program runs. And decades of mature profiling and observability tools.

Now let us install Scala 3 and actually run something. The recommended installer is Coursier. Coursier is a single tool that fetches a compatible java development kit, the Scala 3 compiler, the interactive console, the scala command line interface, and the simple build tool, all in one go. On macOS you install Coursier with brew install coursier, and then you run cs setup. The cs setup command does the heavy lifting. It downloads a recent java development kit, sets up the scala command, and updates your path.

After cs setup finishes, open a new terminal so the updated path takes effect. Then verify the install. Type scala dash dash version and you should see Scala code runner version three dot something. Type scala dash CLI dash dash version to confirm the single file runner is on your path. Type sbt dash dash version to confirm the build tool is too.

Now, Scala gives you three on ramps for running code. Pick the one that matches the size of what you are doing.

The first on ramp is the interactive console, called the repple. The repple is for the question what does this expression do. It is the cheapest possible feedback loop. You type an expression. Scala evaluates it. Scala echoes back the result and the inferred type. You think. You type the next expression. The repple is where you build intuition.

The second on ramp is the scala command line interface, written scala dash CLI. The scala command line interface is for a single file you want to keep around. You write your code in a file called something dot scala, and you run it with scala dash CLI run, followed by the filename. If your script needs a library, you add a special comment at the top of the file declaring the dependency, and the scala command line interface fetches it for you. This is the perfect tool for personal scripts and small experiments.

The third on ramp is the simple build tool, spelled S B T. The simple build tool is for anything that has more than one file, or that you want to build, test, and package seriously. The simple build tool is what Apache Spark itself uses. It manages dependencies, runs your tests, compiles incrementally as you save files, and packages your application into a jar archive you can deploy.

Let us see each of these in action. Open the interactive console with the scala command. You will get a prompt that says scala greater than. From there, type print line, in quotes, hello scala three. The repple prints back hello scala three. Now type val name equals, in quotes, ganesh. The repple binds the name and echoes back the inferred type, which is string. Now type s, in quotes, hello dollar sign name. That returns the string hello ganesh. The lower case s prefix on the string is called a string interpolator. It tells the compiler that the dollar sign placeholders inside the quotes should be replaced with the values of the matching variables. When you are done, type colon q u i t to exit. Colon h e l p shows all the repple commands.

Three details worth registering from that session. First, val bound a value, and the repple knew its type without us writing it down. That is type inference in action. Second, the s prefixed string is a compile time feature, not a runtime function call. The compiler rewrites the interpolated string at compile time. There is no string formatting cost at runtime. Third, the colon prefixed commands are repple commands, not Scala expressions. They are how you talk to the repple itself.

Next, the scala command line interface. For anything longer than two lines you put the code in a file. Create a file called hello dot scala. Inside it, write at sign main, def hello, open paren, close paren, colon Unit equals, and on the next line, indented, print line, in quotes, hello from scala dash CLI. To run the file, type scala dash CLI run hello dot scala. The output is hello from scala dash CLI.

Two new shapes appeared in that example. The first is the at sign main annotation. The at sign main annotation marks an entry point. Whatever method carries this annotation becomes the program's main method. This replaces the older Scala 2 boilerplate where you had to extend a special trait. The second shape is the Unit return type. Unit is Scala's equivalent of void in Java or C. It is a real type, with exactly one inhabitant, written as a pair of empty parentheses. A method whose result type is Unit is a method you call for its side effect, not for its return value.

Now, the simple build tool. For a real project, the simple build tool gives you a directory layout, dependency management, incremental compilation, and a test runner. You will meet it properly in notebook fifteen when this track bridges to Spark. For now, the one command scaffold is enough. Type sbt new scala slash scala three dot g eight. That command pulls a template from the internet, asks for a project name, and creates a directory with the standard layout. There is a build dot sbt file at the root that holds your project configuration. There is a project subdirectory with build metadata. And there is a src subdirectory that contains main slash scala for production code and test slash scala for your tests. To compile and run the project, type sbt run inside the directory.

One last topic before we wrap up. Scala 3 supports two equivalent surface syntaxes for code blocks. The first is significant indentation, where the body of a method or block is marked by indentation, just like in Python. The second is brace style, where the body is wrapped in curly braces. Both styles parse to exactly the same tree. They produce identical bytecode. You can choose per file. This track will use indentation style by default because it is the idiomatic Scala 3 direction and it removes visual noise. When you read Spark's source code, you will see brace style, because Spark itself still targets Scala 2 point thirteen for now. Both shapes are valid Scala. Pick whichever reads better in the file you are editing.

Comments follow the conventions you know from Java and C. Two forward slashes start a single line comment that runs to the end of the line. Slash star starts a block comment that runs until the matching star slash. There is also a special form, slash star star, which begins a ScalaDoc comment. ScalaDoc comments document public application programming interfaces, and tools like scaladoc turn them into HTML reference documentation, much like javadoc does in the Java world.

That is the on ramp. You now have Scala 3 installed. You can poke at it in the interactive console. You can run a single file with the scala command line interface. And you can scaffold a real project with the simple build tool.

Notebook two starts the language itself. We will look at val versus var in depth. We will look at the type system that you mostly cannot see, because the compiler infers it for you. And we will look at a small but important shift in mindset. In Scala, if, while, and for are not statements. They are expressions. They return values. That small change has surprisingly large consequences for how the rest of the language is shaped, and it is where the real learning begins.
